From 2acbfc9bb17be68941fd9e45d923d3e98e662511 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 01:08:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cron): rerun an attempt cut off by a restart once, right
 after recovery [daveey]

A cron run in flight when the gateway restarts (every core update) was lost:
the next process's recover_interrupted_executions booked its ledger row
`unknown`, interrupted_sessions closed the session row, and nothing ran the job
again until its next scheduled slot (a one-shot never).

Signal (narrowest the code already records), all three required:
- the recovery pass is the in-process ticker's FIRST startup recovery for that
  home in this process (scheduler_provider start(), single-profile and
  multiplexed, via _startup_recovery_scope -> executions.startup_recovery());
  the periodic dead-owner reap, the cronjob tool, the external-worker waiter,
  a ticker re-entered mid-life (restart_if_dead; the chronos fallback, whose
  provider start() records its own startup pass) and a
  Desktop multiplexed ticker's startup pass over a profile its gate hands to
  a live gateway only settle the ledger, so a killed `hermes cron run` under
  a live scheduler is not rerun;
- recover_interrupted_executions' dead-owner branch, i.e. rows it settles with
  _OWNER_GONE_REASON (owner pid gone or its start-time fingerprint changed);
  the live-but-wedged branch is excluded (a restart-safe worker that outlived
  the restart may still be running);
- the job's fire claim is gone or names a provably dead process
  (scheduler_ownership._claim_owner_is_dead): an external worker dying under a
  live scheduler is that scheduler's to settle (scheduler_worker_failure).
No shutdown-reason file is consulted: a SIGKILL/OOM'd gateway writes none. An
explicit HERMES_MACHINE_ID makes claim death unprovable, so such hosts never
rerun; a reused pid likewise fails safe (no rerun). The chronos provider's own
start() keeps "never requeue" (it reaps without rerunning).

cron/restart_rerun.py:
- recurring job: an extra fire with the markers trigger_job writes
  (next_run_at = manual_run_at = now, natural slot in manual_run_restore_at),
  so the rerun spends no occurrence, books no missed run, and the job keeps
  its normal schedule. The cut-off attempt never completed, so it is not
  counted against repeat.times either;
- one-shot: re-armed for now with claim_dispatch's spent repeat slot given back
  (also when stop bookkeeping already retired it as completed during the
  attempt); a run-now of a still-future one-shot reruns as a run-now;
- no failure is counted (no mark_job_run; failure_streak untouched);
- gateway log: `cron: rescheduled <job_id> (execution <id>) after restart
  interruption` (WARNING, cron.jobs logger); skips log one INFO line with the
  reason.

Loop guard: the job carries restart_rerun={execution_id, at}. The same
execution is never rescheduled twice, and an attempt claimed at/after an
unsettled reschedule (the rerun itself, cut off again) is not rescheduled, so a
job that takes the gateway down runs at most once extra. Any real outcome
(mark_job_run without shutdown_interrupted) settles the marker; stop
bookkeeping does not.

Not rerun: job deleted; paused (is_job_runnable); edited since the attempt
was claimed (edited_at is stamped by update_job when an update touches an
authored field from job_definition.JOB_DEFINITION_FIELDS or enabled/state/
next_run_at, by rearm_oneshot, and by a definition import that changes an
authored field or repeat.times, so edit, pause, resume, run-now, re-arm and a
changed shipped definition count; bookkeeping writes such as
last_delivery_error/monitor_state and a re-shipped identical definition do
not); a scheduler silent for over RESTART_WINDOW_SECONDS (30 min, measured from
the attempt's last sign of life: claim, fire-claim heartbeat or stop
bookkeeping), which was stopped rather than restarted and is left to the job's
missed-run policy; recurring jobs retired by their repeat limit; one-shots
completed before the attempt; an attempt with an admission journal (canonical
owner fire: journal recovery observes the receipt and must never be raced by a
second submit); an attempt that is no longer the job's newest. A busy fire
fence skips with its own INFO line.

Out of scope (per card): a run whose thread unwinds before the process exits
is booked `failed` ("Interrupted by gateway shutdown before terminal
completion.") by finish_interrupted_run and never reaches recovery; it is not
rerun. Hub/profile ledgers since 2026-10-25 show only `unknown` rows for
restart interruptions. Known gap (fails safe, no double run): if a Desktop
ticker's periodic reap settles the row while the gateway is between processes,
the new gateway finds nothing to rerun.

Proof
- tests/cron/test_restart_rerun.py, 32 tests driving real claim -> ledger ->
  owner death -> recovery, incl. the real InProcessCronScheduler.start()
  startup path (single + multiplex), a non-startup reap, a mid-life ticker
  re-entry, a chronos fallback, and Desktop profiles its gate admits, rejects
  or fails on. RED on
  c9274695b634 (test file only): 30 failed, 4 passed (22 no-rerun controls fail on the missing
  startup_recovery API; the first version, before that API, failed 13 rerun
  behaviours on their assertions with every control passing). GREEN: 34 passed.
- Mutation (sdlc-review mutants.py, 40 mutants over every added condition,
  incl. the startup gate, both provider wrappers, the profile gate and the
  once-per-home set, the chronos mark): 39 killed. Survivor: `claimed >= rescheduled_at` -> `>`
  (equivalent: a rerun is claimed strictly after its reschedule).
- Existing suites: tests/cron/ + tests/{tools,hermes_cli,gateway}/*cron*:
  261 files (+ chronos and web_server cron profile tests), 2321 passed,
  0 failed, 2 skipped (hermes-core-wt test on the hub). scripts/check
  --base origin/main: 16 checks ok.
